# Tableau Desktop authoring

The TableauDesktop server drives the copy of Tableau Desktop running on this
computer. Its own instructions cover how to build and edit content. This skill
covers which server to use, setup, and data apps.

## Desktop or Cloud

- **Desktop:** the user means a workbook open in Tableau Desktop, or a local
  file they want to open there. Use the TableauDesktop tools, such as
  `{{ "TableauDesktop__get-desktop-state" | tool }}`.
- **Cloud or Server:** the user means content published to a Tableau Cloud or
  Tableau Server site. Use the hosted Tableau server and its skills, not this one.
- If the request could mean either, ask the user which one.

If no TableauDesktop tools are available at all, tell the user that Tableau
Desktop authoring needs Claude Code or Codex running on the computer where
Tableau Desktop runs. Don't name or call Desktop tools in that case.

## Several Desktops open

If more than one Tableau Desktop is open, call
`{{ "TableauDesktop__list-instances" | tool }}`, ask the user which one to use
if it isn't clear, and pass that instance's session to the other tools.

## Editing cached workbook XML

Some tools write workbook XML to a cache file and return its path. Change that
file with `{{ "TableauDesktop__read-cached-xml" | tool }}` and
`{{ "TableauDesktop__write-cached-xml" | tool }}`, not with your own file
tools. The cache is outside the user's project, so file edits there ask for
permission every time.

## When the server can't reach Tableau Desktop

Walk the user through these steps in order, stopping once it works:

1. In Tableau Desktop, select Help > Settings and Performance > Enable External
   API Access (Requires Restart). If this setting is unavailable, they need
   their Tableau admin.
2. Restart Tableau Desktop.
3. Check that Tableau Desktop is the most recent public release or later.
{% if platform == "claude" %}
4. Run `/mcp` and choose **Reconnect** for the TableauDesktop server. After the
   server fails, Claude Code doesn't try it again for 15 minutes on its own.
{% else %}
4. Start a new task, so Codex starts the TableauDesktop server again.
{% endif %}
5. If the server never starts, install Node.js 22.7.5 or later, make sure
   `npx` is on the `PATH`{% if platform == "claude" %} that Claude Code sees{% endif %},
   and try step 4 again.

If the user set up a Tableau Desktop MCP server by hand before installing this
plugin, tell them to remove it, so only one server drives Desktop.

## Data apps

Both servers have a `scaffold-data-app` tool. Pick by where the app goes:

- **Into the workbook open in Desktop:** use
  `{{ "TableauDesktop__scaffold-data-app" | tool }}`. It needs a data app
  folder. If it says the folder isn't set, ask the user to set
  {% if platform == "claude" %}the plugin's **Data app folder** option{% else %}the `TABLEAU_DATA_APP_WORKSPACE_DIR` environment variable to an absolute path{% endif %}, then try again.
- **Published to Tableau Cloud or Server:** use the hosted Tableau server's
  `scaffold-data-app` and the tableau-data-app-authoring skill.
